Tuxlang Recent Updates 1.3

1. VM Performance Improvements
- Tagged-value hot paths added for locals/globals
- Fast opcode dispatch path for hot loop instructions
- Large-loop benchmark improved from ~21-15ms to ~5-7ms range depending on system load

3. Concurrency Runtime Upgrade
- Replaced unbounded per-task goroutine spawning with shared worker-runtime queue
- Improved stability under high task fan-out

4. Reliability Stuff
- Added truncated-bytecode operand guards in VM loops
- Added panic-safe equality fallback behavior
- Added parser token-read safety limit for pathological inputs
- Added core execution limits support (op/time budget APIs in VM)

5. GUI Cross-Platform Consistency
- Linux/macOS use native GUI path only
- Windows event output flow aligned to JSON result contract
- Added GUI decode robustness for noisy output lines

6. Fuzzing Improvements
- Added fuzz tests in Tests/fuzz_test.go
  - FuzzParserDoesNotPanic
  - FuzzPipelineDoesNotPanic

7. New Built-in Module: tuxiler
- Added stdlib module `tuxiler`
- `tuxiler.build(targetPath, outputPath?)` packages a `.tux` program into a native Binary depending on the OS
- `tuxiler.self_build(outputPath?)` packages the currently running script
- `build` can resolve target files in nested folders from the current working directory
- Packaging embeds source into the runtime executable
- Native target detection is automatic from the running runtime OS/arch

8. Runtime Script Directives
- Added script directives (plain line or `//` comment):
  - `exit_window: true|false`
  - `show_console: true|false`
- `exit_window` is cross-platform and waits for Enter only in interactive terminals
- `show_console` is cross-platform runtime behavior (suppresses stdout/stderr when false)
- On Windows packaged binaries, `show_console` also sets PE subsystem (console vs GUI)
